WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:07.920
This video uses three examples from the chicken
genome to illustrate common uses of clone

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.849
placement data:
- Find genomic clones that contain a gene

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:16.129
of interest
- Use clone placements to recognize a genomic

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:22.240
region with potential mis-assembly
- Use clone placements to recognize potential

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.330
structural variations

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:31.189
A companion video that focuses on clone FTP
content is linked at the end of this video.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:37.040
Very briefly, clone placements are created
by aligning end and insert sequences on genome

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.130
assemblies annotated at NCBI.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:46.110
To generate a clone placement for end sequences,
both ends (forward and reverse) must be placed

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.260
on the same assembly molecule.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.940
For the first example,
let's find clone placements in the region

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.460
of the chicken WDR3 gene.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.330
I'm starting in the Genome Data viewer, but
if you happened to be in a Gene record, you

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:05.900
could link from there to the same GDV display
we are about to see.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.340
I'll select the previous chicken assembly,
Gallus-gallus 5.0, but that is simply because

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:17.320
our three examples better illustrate the utility
of clone placements in that assembly.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.040
You might want to have a look at the wdr3
gene region in the updated assembly to see

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.070
how it has improved.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:29.329
To get to the region of the WDR3 gene, use
the Search box on the left.

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:32.479
We now see the WDR3 gene model.

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:35.810
The next step is to add tracks for clone placements.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:43.689
From the Tracks menu, mouse over NCBI Recommended
Track Sets then select Assembly Support.

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:49.119
This adds a pre-defined group of tracks, including
clone placements for the 3 libraries with

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:52.630
the largest number of placements.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:57.240
Clones with unique concordant placements are
displayed as solid blue lines.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.469
Unique discordant clones display as a solid
red line and multiple discordant clones display

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:05.439
as dotted red line.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:10.570
This clone also has a grey background, indicating
that not all end sequences associated with

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:13.610
the clone support this placement.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:21.440
If you wanted to, you could add other clone
placement tracks in the Genomic Clones set.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.390
And notice that you can upload other data,
such as one of the GFF files on the Clone

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.410
FTP site.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:34.130
Okay, we're now ready to identify good clones
for the WDR3 gene.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:38.190
I'll adjust the zoom a bit in order to better
see the assembly gap.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:45.670
I'll also add a marker for the gap region.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.820
And I'll need to scroll down to see the clone
tracks.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:56.440
Now you see that the CH261 library includes
several blue clones, those with unique concordant

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:01.720
placements, that contain this assembly gap,
so we've met our first goal of finding genomic

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.580
clones for a gene of interest.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:09.230
Let's move on to the second example, a quick
look at using clone placements to assess the

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:15.340
quality of an assembly, in this case, to recognize
a genomic region with potential mis-assembly.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.380
I've moved over to chicken chromosome Z.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.710
And we see a dramatic change in placements
from unique concordant to discordant at about

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.760
72 megabases.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.490
This change ocurrs in all 3 libraries, indicating
a likely mis-assembly in the region.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:38.760
The DNA source for this Gallus_gallus-5.0
assembly is the same as in the CH261 library,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.640
but different in the two other libraries,
further supporting a mis-assembly here.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.550
In the final example, we look at clone placements
that indicate possible structural variation.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.560
I've moved over to chromosome 21.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:04:00.090
And here we see a few smaller regions in the
J_AC and J_AD libraries with placements of

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.910
both concordant and discordant clones.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:08.310
This mixture is suggestive of structural variation,
but additional analyses are needed to verify

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:15.550
this, including close evaluation of boundaries
and sequencing of clones to narrow down locations.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:21.289
Notice that the end clones from the CH261
library have only concordant placements in

00:04:21.289 --> 00:04:22.569
these regions.

00:04:22.569 --> 00:04:27.070
Remember that the source sequence for CH261
is different from the other two libraries,

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.629
leaving open the possibility of genomic variation
in these regions.

00:04:31.629 --> 00:04:33.509
That concludes this tutorial.

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:39.480
Please use the Support Center link found on
most NCBI pages for your questions and feedback;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:40.509
we would love to hear from you.

